{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FN1tyRa5vQyy8TZyw5rsxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. They derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. Key components:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n**Current State**: \n- Specification phase is largely complete for the first batches\n- No code has been implemented yet (implementation pending)\n- PRDs are complete for: testutils, sys, format, ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls (core), du, ls (extended)\n- Use cases written for all releases\n- Test suites written for all releases\n\n**Roadmap**:\n- rel01.0 (in_progress): ts - timestamp stdin lines. Use case rel01.0-uc001-ts is in_progress\n- rel01.1 (pending): wc, cat, sponge\n- rel02.0 (pending): ls core, du\n- rel02.1 (pending): ls extended\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel01.0, which needs the differential testing harness in pkg/testutils before I can implement and test the ts utility. Since I can only create one task, I need to start with pkg/testutils—the foundational piece that unblocks everything else in this release.\n\nLooking at what the harness needs to cover: test case structures, binary execution, comparison and reporting, normalization hooks, and file-state comparison. Given the constraints around task sizing and the principle of keeping types and implementation together when they're small enough, I'm scoping the first task to handle the core implementation of these five requirements with their acceptance criteria, while keeping the design decisions focused on the essential patterns needed for differential testing.\n\nFor the file structure, I need to follow the naming convention properly—the primary file should be named after the main exported type rather than the package name, so if `DiffTest` is the central struct, that determines the filename.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the task structure to cover all five requirement groups within the line budget, making sure the implementation task encompasses the struct definition, the binary execution logic, comparison and reporting mechanisms, normalization hooks, and file-state comparison functionality. I need to be careful not to invent design decisions beyond what the PRD specifies. Looking at the constraint again, I can only have exactly 1 task, not multiple. Let me also verify the line budget for the pkg/testutils implementation—accounting for the DiffTest struct, the main test runner function, binary execution helpers, comparison logic, normalization functions, and file-state comparison, I'm looking at roughly 280 lines total, which should fit within the scope.\n\nSince this is a single cohesive package focused on the DiffTest type, creating just one file (pkg/testutils/difftest.go) makes sense rather than splitting across multiple files. The PRD says \"touching no more than 5-7 files,\" not that I must touch that many. For an implementation-only task like this, a follow-up task would handle the corresponding tests.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the acceptance criteria to make sure the implementation covers everything: the RunDiffTests function needs to work with a simple slice of DiffTest structs, output formatting for failures needs to show args/stdin/stdout/stderr/exit codes, TimestampNormalizer should handle strftime patterns, file expectations need to be compared properly, timeouts need clear messaging, and the whole thing needs to pass golangci-lint without warnings.\n\nSince we're splitting implementation from tests, I should focus the implementation task on getting the API and compilation right rather than test outcomes, though the PRD acceptance criteria still guide what needs to be built.\n\nDefining the task structure... Writing the test framework...\n\nI need to implement a TimestampNormalizer that strips out common timestamp patterns like strftime formats, ISO-8601 datetimes, and elapsed time strings, replacing them with a placeholder so timestamps don't cause false test failures. Then I'll add file-state comparison for utilities like sponge, cp, and mv that primarily work with files rather than stdout—allowing tests to specify expected file paths and content, comparing them byte-for-byte just like stdout divergences. The primary source file will be difftest.go following the project convention.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm using os/exec.CommandContext with a configurable timeout to enforce execution limits, defining Normalize as a function type that takes and returns byte slices, and applying multiple normalizers in sequence through a DiffTest field. The acceptance criteria require the package to compile cleanly and expose an API that lets cmd packages define a DiffTest slice and invoke RunDiffTests with a Go binary and reference implementation.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the file-state comparison—I need to understand how DiffTest will compare expected file states after a binary runs, whether it's checking file contents, permissions, or both. The test creates an output file, both binaries write to it, and we compare the output files. So the DiffTest needs fields for expected file paths and content.\n\nLooking at prd001 R5:\n- R5.1: Must support utilities whose primary output is a file rather than stdout (sponge, cp, mv). For these, DiffTest must allow specifying expected file content after execution alongside expected stdout/stderr.\n- R5.2: Must compare expected file content byte-for-byte and report divergence in the same format as stdout divergence.\n\nSo the DiffTest struct needs fields for specifying file expectations. Let me include this in the requirements.\n\nI think this looks good. Let me also double-check the requirement count: 5 requirements. In range of 5-8. Design decisions: 3. In range of 3-5. Acceptance criteria: 6. In range of 5-8.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm looking at a single file in the difftest package since this is a focused library. The constraint about file naming means I need to avoid calling it testutils.go—instead I should name it after the primary exported type like DiffTest. I also need to create the pkg/testutils directory since it's spec complete but not yet implemented. I'm making sure the stitch agent has enough context to understand what needs to be built—the purpose of pkg/testutils, the exact API contract, and which file to create—since it won't have access to this conversation or broader project context. The required_reading and detailed requirements should give it everything needed to implement this correctly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71783,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71783},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b468666-2ca0-416c-ac39-87c2a8a88e88","uuid":"fc86e7d4-32b6-499f-b616-112b659c86b0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FN1tyRa5vQyy8TZyw5rsxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that derives formal specs from C/Perl source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies correctness via differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with:\n- `cmd/` — per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness (shared by all utilities)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions (ls, du, find, xargs)\n- `pkg/format/` — output formatting (ls, du)\n- `magefiles/` — Mage build targets bridging human spec phase and automated synthesis\n\n**Current state**: All specification artifacts (PRDs, use cases, test suites) exist for releases 01.0 through 02.1. No Go implementation code exists yet. The pipeline is at the transition point from \"specify\" to \"synthesize.\"\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) — single use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (timestamp stdin lines via ts). This requires `pkg/testutils` (the differential testing harness) and `cmd/ts` (the utility).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel01.0**. Its sole use case (`rel01.0-uc001-ts`) depends on two components:\n1. **pkg/testutils** — the differential testing harness (shared infrastructure, no dependencies on other pkg/ components)\n2. **cmd/ts** — the ts utility implementation (depends on pkg/testutils for verification)\n\nPer canonical ordering: libraries before consumers → `pkg/testutils` must be built first. Per P2: implementation and tests are separate tasks. The DiffTest struct and Normalize type are small enough to combine with the implementation (steps 1+2 of the three-task decomposition).\n\nThe single task to propose is the `pkg/testutils` implementation covering prd001 R1–R5.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils DiffTest harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (full PRD for the differential testing harness -- all requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component description, project structure, design decisions)\n      - go.mod (module path for import references)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, Normalize type, RunDiffTests function, comparison and reporting logic, TimestampNormalizer, file-state comparison\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define the DiffTest struct with fields for Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte -- nil means EOF immediately), Env ([]string -- nil means inherit test process environment), ExitCode (int -- expected exit code), Normalize ([]Normalize -- applied in order before comparison; nil or empty means no normalization), WorkDir (string -- optional working directory; empty means per-test temp directory), and file expectation fields for utilities whose output is a file. Define the Normalize function type as func([]byte) []byte. Per prd001-testutils R1.1-R1.4.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that iterates over the test slice and runs each as a named subtest via t.Run. For each test: invoke both binaries with identical args, stdin, and environment; capture stdout, stderr, and exit code independently; impose a configurable timeout (default 10 seconds) on each invocation; create a per-test temporary directory when WorkDir is empty. Must not write output during passing tests. Per prd001-testutils R2.1-R2.5.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison and divergence reporting: apply the DiffTest Normalize functions in slice order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison. Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte, normalized stderr byte-for-byte, and exit codes exactly. On any divergence, report via t.Errorf with the DiffTest args, stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), reference binary stdout, Go binary stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, and both exit codes. Per prd001-testutils R3.1-R3.6.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement TimestampNormalizer as an exported Normalize function that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns -- syslog format ('Feb 19 12:34:56'), ISO-8601 datetime, and HH:MM:SS elapsed time -- with a fixed placeholder string (e.g., 'TIMESTAMP'). This normalizer is used by cmd/ts differential tests. Per prd001-testutils R4.1-R4.3.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison: DiffTest must support specifying an output file path and expected content after execution. After both binaries run, compare the output file content byte-for-byte. Report divergence in the same format as stdout divergence (showing expected vs actual file content). Per prd001-testutils R5.1-R5.2.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Primary source file is difftest.go -- the project convention requires naming the file after the primary exported type (DiffTest), not the package name. testutils/testutils.go is forbidden.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use os/exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout for binary invocation to enforce the configurable timeout per prd001-testutils R2.3. Default timeout is 10 seconds.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Normalize is a named exported type: type Normalize func([]byte) []byte. The DiffTest.Normalize field is a slice ([]Normalize) applied in order per prd001-testutils R4.3. When nil or empty, no normalization is applied.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/testutils/difftest.go compiles without errors: go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"The exported API allows a cmd/ package to define a []DiffTest slice and call RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) from a TestXxx function with no other setup per prd001-testutils AC1.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer is exported and replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with a fixed placeholder string per prd001-testutils AC3.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"DiffTest supports file expectation fields so that a test specifying expected file content correctly triggers comparison after binary execution per prd001-testutils AC4.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"A binary invocation exceeding the timeout causes the subtest to fail with a clear timeout message rather than hanging per prd001-testutils AC5.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint per prd001-testutils AC6.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71783,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71783},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b468666-2ca0-416c-ac39-87c2a8a88e88","uuid":"8366dbef-5a3f-40a1-968e-1211a5035553"}
